.TH BASEFWX 1 "August 2026" "BaseFWX 3.8.0-dev1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
basefwx \- command-line cryptographic codec tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
basefwx [global flags] <command> [args]

basefwx fwxaes-enc <file> [--password <password>] [--out <path>]
basefwx fwxaes-dec <file> [--password <password>] [--out <path>]
basefwx fwxaes-live-enc <file|-> [--password <password>] [--out <path|->]
basefwx fwxaes-live-dec <file|-> [--password <password>] [--out <path|->]
basefwx identify <file>
basefwx completion bash
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
basefwx is the command-line frontend for the BaseFWX cryptographic codec
library.
It encrypts files, decodes BaseFWX containers, inspects container
metadata, runs reversible text and binary encodings, and processes
packetized live streams.
.PP
The retired media carrier commands are not part of a default build.
See
.B RETIRED
.B MEDIA
.B COMMANDS
below.
.PP
The CLI is packaged separately from the shared library.
Installing the basefwx package pulls in libbasefwx4 automatically;
applications that only need the library can depend on libbasefwx4 or
libbasefwx-dev without installing the CLI.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.B --verbose, -v
Show additional routing and build information where a command supports
it.
.TP
.B --no-log
Suppress non-essential log output.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable styled terminal output.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print version and feature information.
.SH GENERAL COMMANDS
.TP
.B help
Print command usage.
.TP
.B version
Print build version and enabled feature flags.
.TP
.B completion bash
Print a bash completion script.
.TP
.BI "info " file.fwx
Print BaseFWX container information.
.TP
.BI "identify " file
Print a formatted container summary.
.TP
.BI "probe " file
Alias for identify.
.SH FILE ENCRYPTION
.TP
\fBfwxaes-enc \fIfile\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR]\fR
Encrypt a file with the fwxAES container path.
.TP
\fBfwxaes-dec \fIfile\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR]\fR
Decrypt a fwxAES container after authentication succeeds.
.TP
.B fwxaes-heavy-enc, fwxaes-heavy-dec
Aliases for the heavy fwxAES mode.
.TP
.B fwxaes-stream-enc, fwxaes-stream-dec
Stream-oriented fwxAES file modes.
.TP
.B fwxaes-live-enc, fwxaes-live-dec
Packetized live stream encryption and decryption.
Use "-" for stdin or stdout where supported.
.TP
\fBan7 \fIfile.fwx\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR] [--keep-input] [--force-any]\fR
Apply the reversible AN7 stealth transform to a file that is already
encrypted.
Unlike the commands above, an7 never reads the password as a positional
argument.
It takes the input path, then only the flags listed here, and the
password arrives through
.B --password
or
.BR -p .
Keys come from Argon2id over a fresh 16-byte salt at time cost 5, 128
MiB, and parallelism 4.
The payload is rewritten in 1 MiB chunks, then an encrypted trailer and
a 64-byte footer are appended.
The input must end in .fwx unless
.B --force-any
is given.
Without
.B --out
the result is written beside the input as "data" followed by ten random
digits, and an
.B --out
path that names an existing directory receives that generated name.
The input file is removed on success unless
.B --keep-input
is given.
.TP
\fBdean7 \fIfile\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR] [--keep-input]\fR
Reverse an AN7 file and print the restored path.
The original name, extension, and SHA-256 digest are read from the
encrypted trailer, and the digest is checked before the command reports
success.
The password is supplied the same way as for an7, and
.B --force-any
is rejected here.
Without
.B --out
the restored file is written beside the input under its recovered
original name.
The input file is removed on success unless
.B --keep-input
is given.
.TP
.B b512file-enc, b512file-dec, pb512file-enc, pb512file-dec
Password-backed file codec modes.
.SH TEXT AND BINARY CODECS
.TP
.B b64-enc, b64-dec
Base64 text encode/decode helpers.
.TP
.B n10-enc, n10-dec, n10file-enc, n10file-dec
BaseFWX n10 text and file encoders.
.TP
.B hash512
SHA-512 hash helper command.
.TP
.B b512-enc, b512-dec, pb512-enc, pb512-dec
Password-backed text codec modes.
.SH BENCHMARK COMMANDS
The bench commands are measurement helpers, not data-processing
commands.
Each one prints
.BI BENCH_NS= n
with the median wall-clock nanoseconds of one timed iteration.
Some commands also print a verification or throughput line.
All leave the named input in place, but only bench-an7, bench-dean7,
bench-b512file, and bench-pb512file copy or seed per-worker inputs in
owner-only temporary directories.
The fwxAES commands read the named input into memory, and bench-live
reads it directly.
.PP
Iteration and thread counts come from the environment:
.B BASEFWX_BENCH_WARMUP
(default 2),
.B BASEFWX_BENCH_ITERS
(default 50),
.B BASEFWX_BENCH_WORKERS
(default the detected hardware concurrency), and
.B BASEFWX_BENCH_PARALLEL
set to 0, false, off, or no to force a single worker.
Every bench command except bench-hash also accepts the master-key flags
described in
.B KEY
.B OPTIONS
below.
.TP
\fBbench-text \fImethod text-file\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR]\fR
Time one encode plus decode round trip of the file's text.
.I method
is b64, n10, b512, or pb512.
The b512 and pb512 methods take a password and also accept --kdf and
--pbkdf2-iters.
.TP
.BI "bench-hash " "method text-file"
Time hashing of the file's text.
.I method
is hash512.
This command takes no flags.
.TP
.BI "bench-fwxaes " "file password"
Time one in-memory fwxAES encrypt plus decrypt round trip over the file
bytes.
This one is single-threaded and ignores the worker setting.
.TP
.BI "bench-fwxaes-par " "file password"
Time the same fwxAES round trip spread across worker threads.
It prints an extra
.BI THROUGHPUT_GiBps= value
.RI WORKERS= n
line when the measurement is usable.
.TP
.BI "bench-an7 " "file password"
Time an7 over a per-worker fwxAES-encrypted seed built from the input.
.TP
.BI "bench-dean7 " "file password"
Time dean7 over a per-worker AN7 seed built the same way.
.TP
.BI "bench-live " "file password"
Time a packetized live encrypt plus decrypt round trip in memory and
verify the restored length.
In the C++ CLI the worker count is capped to fit the input size and
available memory, and a warning names the reduced count.
.B BASEFWX_BENCH_MEMORY_LIMIT_BYTES
overrides that C++ memory budget.
The Java command uses the configured worker count without this extra cap
and prints
\fBBENCH_VERIFIED_BYTES=\fIn\fR.\fR
.TP
.BI "bench-b512file " "file password"
Time a b512 file encode plus decode round trip on a per-worker copy of
the input.
.TP
.BI "bench-pb512file " "file password"
Time the same round trip through the pb512 file codec.
.PP
Both C++ and Java bench-fwxaes-par print
.BI THROUGHPUT_GiBps= value
.RI WORKERS= n
when the measurement is usable.
Java bench-live also prints the verified-byte line described above.
.SH RETIRED COMPATIBILITY COMMANDS
b256, A512, Bi512, Uhash513, the jMG media cipher, and the kFM/kFA
carrier codecs are retired.
A default build does not compile or install them, and the commands below
are absent from its help output and completion script.
.PP
They exist only in a compatibility build, configured with
.BR -DBASEFWX_ENABLE_RETIRED_MEDIA=ON ,
and is meant for reading retired data that already exists.
That build preserves the historical commands, formats, and bytes; it
receives security, correctness, and existing-data compatibility fixes
only.
Encrypt new media with
.B fwxaes-enc
instead.
.PP
Because the switch selects what gets compiled, it cannot restore these
commands in an artifact that was built without them.
Use a compatibility build of the same BaseFWX version that wrote the
file.
.TP
.B b256-enc, b256-dec, a512-enc, a512-dec, bi512-enc, uhash513
Historical text codecs retained for exact-byte and decode compatibility.
.TP
\fBjmge \fImedia\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR]\fR
Encode media through the jMG media cipher path.
.TP
\fBjmgd \fImedia\fR [--password \fIpassword\fR] [--out \fIpath\fR]\fR
Decode jMG media output.
.TP
\fBkFMe \fIinput\fR [--out \fIpath\fR] [--bw]\fR
Encode into a strict BaseFWX carrier format.
.TP
\fBkFMd \fIinput\fR [--out \fIpath\fR] [--bw]\fR
Decode a strict BaseFWX carrier.
Plain media that is not a BaseFWX carrier is rejected.
.TP
.B kFAe, kFAd
Deprecated aliases for the PNG-only carrier path.
.SH KEY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--password " password
Provide a password on the command line.
Passworded commands prompt on a TTY when this option is omitted.
.TP
.B --use-master, --no-master
Enable or disable master-key wrapping where a command supports it.
Provision the intended public key, retain metadata, and verify
complete-file recovery independently.
Some file paths replace requested master intent with key availability or
metadata stripping, so command success does not establish that a
recoverable master wrap was written.
Streaming B512 uses STRMOBF1 and still requires the original password
for its internal obfuscation.
The reader does not authenticate that password separately; a matching
master key with a wrong password can produce corrupted output.
For password recovery with an unrelated configured master private key,
use
.BR --no-master .
Enabled master recovery is tried first; payload authentication failure
does not retry the password path.
.TP
.BI "--master-pub " path
Use a master public key file where supported.
.TP
.B --master-autogen
Deprecated and has no effect since 3.7.0.
It only implies
.BR --use-master .
Silent EC master autogeneration was removed.
.TP
.B --allow-embedded-master
Allow embedded master-key metadata where supported.
.TP
.B --strip-meta
Omit file-container metadata where supported.
AES-heavy pb512file authoring rejects this option because its KDF costs
require metadata for recovery.
B512file authoring also rejects it when the input selects the streaming
container, whose metadata carries the required dispatch marker.
.TP
\fB--kdf \fIlabel\fR, --pbkdf2-iters \fIn\fR
Select key-derivation behavior where a command supports it.
